    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and both tests pass. **BUILD SUCCESS.**\n\nThe change is complete. Here's what I did, keeping each piece of logic in the class that owns the relevant data:\n\n1. **`PostcodePolicy`** (owns the postcode-range table) \u2014 added `regionForPostcode(String postcode)`, a reverse lookup returning `Optional<String>` that reuses `REGION_POSTCODE_RANGE`. It returns empty when the postcode is null, non-numeric, or in no known range. Extracted a small `inRange(...)` helper so both `isValidForCity` and the new method share the range-check logic instead of duplicating it.\n\n2. **`LocalityResolver`** (owns the city-to-region table) \u2014 `resolve` now takes `(city, postcode)` and prefers the postcode: `PostcodePolicy.regionForPostcode(postcode).orElseGet(() -> regionForCity(city))`. The former single-arg lookup is preserved as `regionForCity(city)` (still the city-only fallback, and reused by `PostcodePolicy.isValidForCity`).\n\n3. **`OwnerMapper`** \u2014 the `locality` mapping now passes `owner.getCity()` and `owner.getPostcode()`.\n\nThis returns the same region for known cities (Sydney\u2192NSW via either path) but disambiguates same-named cities by postcode \u2014 e.g. an unknown city with postcode `3000` now correctly resolves to `VIC`, which is exactly what `functionalityPostcodePreferredOverCity` asserts. No logic is duplicated: each table stays in one class, and the range/reverse-lookup logic lives where the postcode table lives.",
